package com.linguapro.android

internal object WritingModelsC1 {
    val answers = mapOf(
        "C1-U1" to "The reviewer accepts the pilot's positive outcome but challenges the claim that it demonstrates effectiveness generally. Voluntary participation, a short comparison period and the absence of an equivalent concurrent group limit that inference. In my view, continuing the work is justified because encouraging observations can warrant investigation without establishing causation. The revised report is therefore more credible when it preserves both the improvement and the uncertainty. It should not suggest that the pilot has failed, but neither should enthusiasm for funding convert limited evidence into a stronger conclusion than the design supports.",
        "C1-U2" to "The revised email shows that adapting register need not obscure the practical request. The writer specifies the fault, its effect and the confirmation needed, while addressing an external partner respectfully. The passage presents precision, rather than elaborate ceremony, as the basis of effective professional communication. I find that distinction persuasive: politeness helps preserve the relationship, but responsibility and timing must remain identifiable. This does not establish that one tone suits every audience. It suggests that the choice of tone should support the action and relationship at stake instead of replacing a clear request with formal language alone.",
        "C1-U3" to "The reports evaluate different outcomes over different periods, so describing them as simply positive and negative would create an artificial contradiction. Immediate satisfaction does not necessarily establish sustained use of skills, while weak transfer need not deny that participants valued the sessions. The passage argues for relating the findings without collapsing their measures. In my interpretation, the two reports support separate questions about reception and application. They do not, by themselves, identify why transfer may be limited or determine how the programme should change; those conclusions would require evidence beyond the comparison presented.",
        "C1-U4" to "The revised proposal strengthens its case by addressing the specialist-function objection rather than speculating about opponents' motives. A common core could reduce duplication, while limited modules could preserve relevant differences between teams. The passage also acknowledges that this arrangement would require additional maintenance, preventing the compromise from appearing cost-free. I regard the revision as more persuasive because it explains how coordination might work under the actual constraint. However, the account does not establish that the proposed modules are sufficient or affordable. Taking the objection seriously improves the argument without proving that the resulting design is the best available solution.",
        "C1-U5" to "The mediator makes agreement possible by separating public deadlines from the interests they protect. A staged announcement can address one side's communication need while preserving time for the other's checks. The passage makes honest description of unfinished work a condition of the arrangement, rather than treating the announcement as full implementation. In my view, this condition is central: without it, the compromise could merely conceal the original conflict. The account illustrates a constructive negotiation, but does not guarantee that every deadline dispute can be resolved through staging or that either party can ignore the remaining quality requirements.",
        "C1-U6" to "The student distinguishes an original finding from two increasingly strong interpretations. Tracing the citation reveals that the study used narrower language and acknowledged limitations absent from the later claim. The passage does not reject either circulation or commentary automatically; it asks what each source can legitimately support. I consider the student's approach defensible because it separates evidence from discussion about evidence. Nevertheless, reaching the original source does not remove the need to assess its methods and relevance. It prevents one specific attribution error without certifying every conclusion in the study or the student's essay.",
        "C1-U7" to "The dispute concerns the scope of participation as much as the preferred policy. Residents understood consultation as potential influence over the broader choice, whereas the council treated that choice as settled. The revised introduction clarifies which decisions remain open and how responses will be used. In my judgement, this improves accountability by reducing the gap between invited participation and actual influence. It does not show that the consultation is sufficient or that residents should accept the settled decision. The clarification makes the disagreement more accurately described, while leaving its legitimacy and outcome open to further evaluation.",
        "C1-U8" to "The editor removes inflated modifiers but retains the qualification that changes how the finding should be interpreted. The passage therefore distinguishes concision from indiscriminate shortening: fewer words are useful only when they preserve the important action and its limits. I read the revision as an improvement in informational precision, rather than as evidence that long sentences or qualifications are inherently poor writing. Its success depends on selecting what carries meaning in this particular opening. A different passage might need more explanation, especially if removing detail would leave the reader unable to judge the scope of the work.",
        "C1-U9" to "The communications lead adapts the implications of the update while preserving its facts. A workaround means partial mitigation to engineers, but the customer team initially reads the phrase as assurance that service will be unaffected. The revision makes the temporary restriction visible in both versions. In my view, this is responsible audience adaptation because it clarifies what each group needs to act on without manufacturing different realities. The passage does not imply that every audience needs identical wording. It establishes a boundary: emphasis may change, but a partial solution must not become a claim that the problem has disappeared.",
        "C1-U10" to "The chair treats uncertainty as a design constraint rather than choosing between indefinite delay and unquestioning action. A reversible step addresses the immediate problem, while recorded assumptions and review conditions keep the judgement provisional. The passage presents this as a way of acting without claiming that uncertainty has vanished. I find the approach proportionate, provided that reversal is genuinely possible and the review criteria remain usable. Those conditions are important because recording them does not ensure that they will be followed. The account supports a cautious decision process, not a guarantee that the chosen step will produce the intended outcome.",
        "C1-U11" to "The review brings together processing time, staff experience and user access without converting them into one undifferentiated verdict. Faster administration may coexist with greater staff pressure and continuing access difficulties. The passage's conclusion is therefore about uneven effects, not about evidence cancelling itself out. In my interpretation, preserving those tensions is more informative than declaring the reform uniformly successful or unsuccessful. However, the three sources still do not provide a complete explanation of the differences or establish which costs are acceptable. The synthesis identifies questions for further investigation while respecting the distinct dimensions each source measures.",
        "C1-U12" to "The rewritten summary makes the requested commitment identifiable and connects it to evidence, risk and the cost of delay. It retains detailed assumptions in an annex rather than pretending they are irrelevant. The passage describes an improvement in decision support, not a reduction of all uncertainty. I consider the structure useful because readers can see what approval would authorise and which conditions require attention. Its clarity does not establish that approval is warranted. Directors must still assess the evidence and assumptions, and a concise recommendation should remain open to challenge rather than acquire authority merely through its presentation.",
        "C1-U13" to "The idiom moved the goalposts expresses the manager's frustration, but does not establish that the customer changed the agreed scope. The conversation reveals competing interpretations of the original promise. Returning to the written specification gives the parties a more concrete basis for identifying what was agreed and what needs a new decision. I read the passage as a warning against confusing vivid language with evidence. The idiom can communicate a stance accurately while leaving its factual basis disputed. Even the specification may require interpretation; consulting it improves the discussion without automatically proving one side's accusation.",
        "C1-U14" to "Economical and cheap share a reference to lower cost but invite different evaluations in this context. The committee's word suggests efficiency, whereas the critic's may imply reduced quality. Lower-cost allows the revision to report the comparison without endorsing either judgement. In my view, this is useful when the purpose is description rather than persuasion. It does not make the evaluative words inaccurate in every setting or interchangeable with one another. Their suitability depends on the evidence and intended stance. The passage clarifies that lexical precision includes recognising connotation as well as identifying the object or comparison being described.",
        "C1-U15" to "The speakers initially appear to dispute an effect, but actually apply different criteria for success. One asks whether behaviour changed; the other asks whether the benefit justified its cost. The observed small effect is compatible with disagreement about practical value. I interpret the chair's intervention as useful because it makes the evaluative standard explicit before objections continue. It does not settle whether the intervention should be adopted. The debate still needs evidence about resources, alternatives and the importance of the outcome, so acknowledging an effect must remain distinct from endorsing the policy that produced it.",
        "C1-U16" to "The article's opening label and choice of voices make redevelopment appear preferable before alternatives receive comparable attention. The passage identifies that asymmetry without concluding that redevelopment is necessarily wrong. I consider this distinction essential: analysing framing concerns how authority and emphasis are distributed, whereas evaluating policy also requires substantive evidence about likely effects. The prominence of supportive residents and the treatment of displacement as a minor obstacle can guide interpretation. Yet noticing those choices does not prove that all quoted claims are false or that the unrepresented position is automatically stronger. It identifies a limitation in the article's construction.",
        "C1-U17" to "The revised reply replaces an unexplained judgement with a concrete constraint and alternatives. It states that additional review would affect delivery under the current timetable, while allowing the partner to reconsider priorities. The passage presents diplomacy as precision without unnecessary personal force, rather than as avoidance of disagreement. In my view, that makes negotiation more productive because the objection can be examined. The revision does not demonstrate that the timetable is immovable or that either alternative must be accepted. It clarifies the present position and its consequence, leaving the practical choice open instead of treating the proposal itself as inherently unreasonable.",
        "C1-U18" to "The facilitator separates identical treatment from support adjusted to unequal circumstances. Naming these principles explains why the examples initially seemed inconsistent: the participants were applying different ideas of fairness. The passage does not rank one principle as universally superior. I interpret its contribution as conceptual clarification that makes subsequent judgement more accountable. The team still needs to explain when different treatment addresses a relevant need and when it becomes arbitrary. Merely invoking equity or equality cannot settle that question. The distinction provides a framework for assessing cases, while evidence and reasons remain necessary for applying it responsibly.",
        "C1-U19" to "The ship metaphor turns delays and budget pressure into an image of imminent danger. The passage distinguishes that rhetorical force from evidence of approaching collapse. A literal paraphrase can reveal which conclusions come from the stated facts and which are encouraged by the image. I find this a useful critical method because it makes the metaphor's contribution inspectable without declaring figurative language illegitimate. However, the available evidence does not establish that collapse is impossible either. The appropriate conclusion is narrower: the degree of urgency implied by the image needs support beyond the association the metaphor itself supplies.",
        "C1-U20" to "The initial message equates support for progress with acceptance of one plan, excluding disagreement about means. The revision acknowledges a plausible alternative and explains a comparative preference, making the persuasion more accountable. In my view, this strengthens the argument because the proposal must compete with a real option instead of an obviously unattractive opposite. The passage does not show that the preferred plan is therefore best. Its costs, uncertainty and claimed advantages still require evaluation. Removing a false binary improves the structure of the debate while leaving the substantive decision open to scrutiny by the audience."
    )
}
